From b1aea569d0f6196c56f7d72d4a9a040a16447f3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arya Alikhani <alexanderaryaalikhani@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:09:28 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/17] boringssl: write a fixed extension tail after the
 shuffled middle

Firefox shuffles its built-in ClientHello extensions on every connection
and then appends extensions that are not part of that permutation. Over
QUIC, every Firefox 156 and 157 ClientHello ends with
quic_transport_parameters and encrypted_client_hello, followed by
pre_shared_key when it resumes. Patch 0004 can fix a prefix and shuffle
the remainder, but cannot keep a tail fixed after a shuffled middle.

Add SSL_CTX_set_extension_order_tail. Known IDs in the tail are written
after every other extension, in the given order; only padding and
pre_shared_key follow them. The extensions between a configured prefix
and the tail are shuffled when a prefix is set or permutation is
enabled, and keep the default order otherwise. An ID may not appear in
both the prefix and the tail; each setter rejects an overlap with
SSL_R_DUPLICATE_EXTENSION. Unknown and duplicate IDs are ignored, as in
the prefix.

The permutation is computed once per handshake, so the second
ClientHello after a HelloRetryRequest keeps the order. With GREASE, the
trailing GREASE extension now precedes the tail in both ClientHelloOuter
and the compressed part of ClientHelloInner, which keeps
ech_outer_extensions in the outer order. Without a tail, the writers
produce the same bytes as before.
